{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#1_3617399591","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the same dedicated article, explicitly defining vicarious trauma and saying it covers symptoms and coping/treatment methods; it is relevant but essentially duplicate evidence from the same source.","learned":"Vicarious trauma can involve taking another person's trauma on as one's own, causing emotional/action changes and changes in personal beliefs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006","reason":"Continue the productive focused query to find additional dedicated resources, ideally from distinct sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1687373808#6_2612765895","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1006","rank":3,"snippet":", \"end_char\": 6251.000000 , \"headings\": \"Vicarious Trauma Vicarious Trauma Understanding Vicarious Trauma Symptoms of Vicarious Trauma Vicarious Trauma and Mental Health Self-Care Practices to Reduce Risk of Vicarious Trauma References: Maryanne The GoodTherapy.org Team MissBea Noah \" , \"segment\": \"Therapy is generally recommended to those affected by vicarious trauma. A therapist can provide time and space for individuals to discuss how experiences of trauma survivors have had an impact. In therapy, helping professionals can also learn and build upon <cover>coping skills to address symptoms of vicarious trauma </cover>and develop self-care practices to help prevent future recurrences. Self-Care Practices to Reduce Risk of Vicarious Trauma People in helping professions can take specific steps to reduce the risk of vicarious traumatization. Also, it is widely considered to be essential for those who are frequently exposed to traumatic material to receive appropriate training and supervision. Many mental health professionals also recommend connecting with other professionals who understand the experience of working with trauma. One way helping professionals can reduce their risk of vicarious trauma is to ensure they are making enough time for self-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"2/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1687373808#6_2612765895","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to vicarious trauma, with sections on understanding it, symptoms, mental health, and self-care practices, and the snippet directly discusses therapy, coping skills, supervision, and self-care to prevent or address it.","learned":"Coping with vicarious trauma can include therapy, building coping skills, self-care practices, training, supervision, and connecting with peers who understand trauma work."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006","reason":"This query continues to return dedicated resources that answer both what vicarious trauma is and how to cope with it, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1687373808#5_2612763680","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1006","rank":4,"snippet":"Statistical Manual (which was expanded to include “repeated exposure to aversive details” of a traumatic event), some of those who experience vicarious trauma may also be diagnosed with PTSD. A person with PTSD may be affected by intrusive thoughts about trauma, avoid things that cause memories of a traumatic experience, and have increased physiological arousal/reactivity. Therapy is generally recommended to those affected by vicarious trauma. A therapist can provide time and space for individuals to discuss how experiences of trauma survivors have had an impact. In therapy, helping professionals can also learn and build upon <cover>coping skills to address symptoms of vicarious trauma </cover>and develop self-care practices to help prevent future recurrences. Self-Care Practices to Reduce Risk of Vicarious Trauma People in helping professions can take specific steps to reduce the risk of vicarious traumatization. Also, it is widely considered to be essential for those who are frequently exposed to traumatic material to receive appropriate training and supervision.\" , \"start_char\": 3765.000000 , \"title\": \"Blog Therapy, Therapy, Therapy Blog, Blogging Therapy, Therapy,..\" , \"url\": \"https://www.goodtherapy.org/blog/psychpedia/vicarious-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"3/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1687373808#5_2612763680","relevance":3,"reason":"This segment is from a dedicated GoodTherapy vicarious trauma page and directly addresses coping through therapy, coping skills, self-care, training, and supervision, while also explaining PTSD-like symptoms.","learned":"Vicarious trauma may overlap with PTSD symptoms such as intrusive thoughts, avoidance, and arousal; recommended coping includes therapy, coping skills, self-care, training, and supervision."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006","reason":"Although some duplicates are appearing, the query has produced grade-3 evidence; continue briefly to see if more distinct dedicated resources appear."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1006"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_430320290#0_873128466","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1006","rank":5,"snippet":"in the General Public Treating Compassion Fatigue Feeling Another’s Pain Empathy is a valuable trait for the military, first responders, humanitarian aid workers, health care professionals, therapists, advocates for victims of domestic abuse, moderators of offensive online content, and journalists on the front lines of war and disaster. But the more such individuals open themselves up to others' pain, the more likely they will come to share those victims' feelings of heartbreak and devastation. This sapped ability to <cover>cope with secondary trauma can lead to total exhaustion of one’s mental and physical state. What are the symptoms? Created with Sketch. < g id=\"-small\" stroke=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\" fill=\"none\" fill-rule=\"evenodd\"> Those who regularly experience vicarious trauma </cover>often neglect their own self-care and inner life as they struggle with images and stories that can’t be forgotten. Symptoms of compassion fatigue can include exhaustion, disrupted sleep, anxiety, headaches, stomach upset, irritability, numbness, a decreased sense of purpose, emotional disconnection, self-contempt, and difficulties with personal relationships. What causes compassion fatigue?\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Compassion Fatigue | Psychology Today\" , \"url"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"4/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_430320290#0_873128466","relevance":2,"reason":"The page is about compassion fatigue/secondary trauma rather than being dedicated specifically to vicarious trauma, but it gives related symptoms and notes difficulty coping with secondary trauma among helpers exposed to others' suffering.","learned":"Compassion fatigue/secondary trauma is closely related to vicarious trauma and may involve exhaustion, disrupted sleep, anxiety, irritability, numbness, decreased purpose, emotional disconnection, and relationship difficulties."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious traumatization\" (+ definition \"what is\") (+ cope coping self-care supervision))","reason":"Search the alternate term 'vicarious traumatization' with definition and coping/self-care terms to find additional dedicated sources beyond the first query's duplicates and adjacent compassion-fatigue results."}}
