{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"what is vicarious trauma\" (+ cope coping \"self-care\" manage strategies))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#9_3617412852","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1010","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#9_3617412852\" , \"end_char\": 5324.000000 , \"headings\": \"Vicarious Trauma: When Empathy Goes Too Far & How to Cope with it Vicarious Trauma: When Empathy Goes Too Far & How to <cover>Cope with it What is Vicarious Trauma & </cover>Who is Susceptible to it? Signs and Symptoms of Vicarious Trauma How to Treat Vicarious Trauma Methods To Treat Vicarious Trauma Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (TF-CBT): Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR): Get Professional Help \" , \"segment\": \"EMDR is becoming a rising and sought-after treatment for trauma. It is an effective way to reduce negative feelings, thoughts, and physical sensations associated with your specific trauma. EMDR works by using a series of guided eye movements and other exercises to get you “desensitized” from the traumatic event. Get Professional Help You do not have to let Vicarious Trauma take over your life. Trauma therapy is one of the best ways to cope with the trauma of any kind and prevent future occurrences. So, you may want to consult with a therapist trained in Cognitive ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#0_3617397938","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#1_3617399591","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"9/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#9_3617412852","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated CBT Psychology article on vicarious trauma, with headings explicitly covering what it is, symptoms, treatment methods, and how to cope; the snippet states trauma therapy and EMDR/CBT can help, though it duplicates an already found source.","learned":"Coping/treatment approaches mentioned include trauma therapy, TF-CBT, EMDR, and getting professional help from a therapist."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"coping strategies\" (+ definition signs symptoms))","reason":"Use a concise phrase query for 'coping strategies' plus definition/signs/symptoms to find additional dedicated guides from distinct sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"coping strategies\" (+ definition signs symptoms))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#5_340982832","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1011","rank":1,"snippet":"and seating charts. I was blindsided by the emotional aspect of teaching—I didn’t know how to handle it. Vicarious trauma affects teachers’ brains in much the same way that it affects their students’: The brain emits a fear response, releasing excessive cortisol and adrenaline that can increase heart rate, blood pressure, and respiration, and release a flood of emotions. This biological response can manifest in mental and physical <cover>symptoms such as anger and headaches, or workplace behaviors like missing meetings, lateness, or avoiding certain students, say experts. Yet many teachers are never explicitly taught how to help students who have experienced trauma, let alone address the toll it takes on their own health and personal lives. We reached out to trauma-informed experts and educators around the country to get their recommendations for in-the-moment coping strategies and preventative measures to help teachers process vicarious trauma. </cover>We share their tips below.\" , \"start_char\": 3410.000000 , \"title\": \"When Students Are Traumatized, Teachers Are Too | Edutopia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edutopia.org/article/when-students-are-traumatized-teachers-are-too\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"10/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_168597421#5_340982832","relevance":3,"reason":"The article is dedicated to vicarious trauma in teachers and directly explains its effects/symptoms while presenting expert recommendations for in-the-moment coping strategies and preventive measures.","learned":"Vicarious trauma can trigger fear-response physiology and show up as anger, headaches, avoidance, lateness, or missing meetings; teachers may need coping strategies and preventive measures to process it."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011","reason":"This query is producing direct coping-strategy resources; continue to find more dedicated documents from other sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1148700328#3_2179517896","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1011","rank":2,"snippet":"Those Exposed to Vicarious Trauma? Who Is at Risk of Being Affected by Vicarious Trauma? What Are Some Common Negative Reactions to Vicarious Trauma? Suggestions for Coworkers Suggestions for Supervisors Suggestions for Family Members For More Information About <cover>Vicarious Trauma Education and Awareness Featured Tools Related Resources Prevalence and Risk Factors Related Resources Impact Featured Tools Related Resources \" , \"segment\": \"and can vary from individual to individual, particularly with prolonged exposure. The chart below illustrates the VTT Model. Vicarious traumatization is a negative reaction to trauma exposure and includes a range of psychosocial symptoms. ( In the VTT, the term “vicarious traumatization” is used broadly to include other related terms such as secondary traumatic stress (STS), compassion fatigue (CF), and critical incident stress (CIS). These terms, often used interchangeably, have distinct and overlapping definitions, as illustrated in the Glossary of Terms .) A neutral reaction signifies the ways that an individual's resilience, experiences, support, and coping strategies </cover>manage the traumatic material, not that it has no effect. Vicarious resilience and vicarious transformation are newer concepts reflecting the positive effects of this work. For instance, individuals may draw inspiration from a victim’s resilience that strengthens their "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"11/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1148700328#3_2179517896","relevance":3,"reason":"The document appears to be a dedicated vicarious trauma education/awareness toolkit with sections on who is at risk, reactions, and suggestions for coworkers, supervisors, and family members; the passage defines vicarious traumatization and discusses coping strategies and resilience.","learned":"Vicarious traumatization is a negative reaction to trauma exposure with psychosocial symptoms; related terms include secondary traumatic stress, compassion fatigue, and critical incident stress, and support/coping strategies can help manage traumatic material."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011","reason":"The current query is yielding dedicated resources with definitions and coping/support information, so continue to collect additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_523681915#3_449768456","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1011","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_523681915#3_449768456\" , \"end_char\": 2799.000000 , \"headings\": \"Vicarious traumatization Vicarious traumatization Contents Signs and symptoms Contributing factors Related concepts Mechanism Measurement Addressing See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"somatic <cover>symptoms; sleep difficulties; intrusive imagery; cynicism; sexual difficulties; difficulty managing boundaries with clients; and core beliefs and resulting difficulty in relationships reflecting problems with security, trust, esteem, intimacy, and control. Contributing factors Vicarious trauma, conceptually based in constructivist self-development theory, arises from an interaction between individuals and their situations. This means that the individual helper's personal history (including prior traumatic experiences), coping strategies, </cover>and support network, among other things, all interact with his or her situation (including work setting, the nature of the work s/he does, the specific clientele served, etc.), to give rise to individual expressions of vicarious trauma.\" , \"start_char\": 2011.000000 , \"title\": \"Vicarious traumatization - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicarious_traumatization\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_523681915#3_449768456","relevance":3,"reason":"The page is dedicated to vicarious traumatization, with sections on signs, contributing factors, and addressing it; the snippet provides symptoms and explains that personal history, coping strategies, support networks, and work context influence vicarious trauma.","learned":"Vicarious traumatization may involve somatic symptoms, sleep difficulties, intrusive imagery, cynicism, boundary problems, and changes in beliefs about security, trust, esteem, intimacy, and control; coping strategies and support networks are contributing/moderating factors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011","reason":"The coping-strategies query is yielding multiple dedicated resources, including professional/toolkit and encyclopedic pages, so continue to retrieve more potentially grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1011"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_523681915#4_449769695","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1011","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_523681915#4_449769695\" , \"end_char\": 3555.000000 , \"headings\": \"Vicarious traumatization Vicarious traumatization Contents Signs and <cover>symptoms Contributing factors Related concepts Mechanism Measurement Addressing See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"difficulty managing boundaries with clients; and core beliefs and resulting difficulty in relationships reflecting problems with security, trust, esteem, intimacy, and control. Contributing factors Vicarious trauma, conceptually based in constructivist self-development theory, arises from an interaction between individuals and their situations. This means that the individual helper's personal history (including prior traumatic experiences), coping strategies, </cover>and support network, among other things, all interact with his or her situation (including work setting, the nature of the work s/he does, the specific clientele served, etc.), to give rise to individual expressions of vicarious trauma. This in turn implies the individual nature of responses or adaptations to VT as well as individual ways of coping with and transforming it. Anything that interferes with the helper's ability to fulfill his/her responsibility to assist traumatized clients can contribute to vicarious "}}
