{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1057978851#6_2194081282","relevance":2,"reason":"This is still a law-enforcement mental health resource page rather than the OVC toolkit itself, but it directly defines vicarious trauma as exposure to other people's traumatic experiences and states that organizations can mitigate negative effects by becoming vicarious-trauma-informed.","learned":"Organizations can reduce negative effects of vicarious trauma exposure by becoming vicarious-trauma-informed; the OVC VTT is a key resource but has not yet been retrieved directly."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"trauma-informed\" (+ mitigate mitigating reduce) (+ organization organizational exposure))","reason":"Search for resources about vicarious-trauma-informed organizations and mitigation, which may retrieve the actual OVC toolkit or other dedicated organizational coping/prevention guides."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"trauma-informed\" (+ mitigate mitigating reduce) (+ organization organizational exposure))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_554883146#2_1184737378","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1017","rank":2,"snippet":"of children and adolescents who have been exposed to trauma compared to those who haven’t is different. Children and adolescents without traumatic <cover>exposure can achieve survival and emotional and behavioural control, allowing them to flourish and succeed socially, emotionally and cognitively. However, children and adolescents exposed to trauma find it challenging to achieve at school due to their heightened state of arousal and real or perceived concerns about their safety and security. Adapted from Holt and Jordan, Ohio Department of Education. The benefits of trauma-informed practice in schools Trauma-based educational practices have gained ground over the past 10 years. These practices: improve realisation and recognition among educators of trauma and its impacts on children and adolescents improve educator responses to children and adolescents experiencing trauma limit re-traumatisation of children and adolescents by increasing support and reducing punishments in education settings limit secondary/vicarious trauma among educators by increasing support and professional learning of educators concerning child and adolescent trauma. Trauma-informed models in schools and early learning settings have been shown to reduce </cover>stress, anxiety and depression among children and adolescents. They can also reduce stress and feelings of helplessness in educators when responding to trauma ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1057978851#6_2194081282","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
