{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#5_3617406818","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from a dedicated article titled 'Vicarious Trauma: When Empathy Goes Too Far & How to Cope with it,' with explicit sections on what vicarious trauma is, signs/symptoms, treatment, and coping; the passage lists behavioral/physical symptoms and begins treatment advice, though it duplicates a previously found source.","learned":"Vicarious trauma symptoms may include headaches, stomach pain, exhaustion, isolation, substance use, eating changes, risky behavior, sleep difficulty, procrastination, and problems with concentration, decision-making, and memory; coping begins with stepping back from trauma exposure and prioritizing oneself."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"american counseling association\" (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies symptoms))","reason":"Search for American Counseling Association resources, since earlier evidence cited ACA definitions and it may provide authoritative dedicated guidance on vicarious trauma and coping/self-care."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"american counseling association\" (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies symptoms))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1638929939#1_3233078716","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1025","rank":1,"snippet":"may inadvertently neglect their own mental wellness. Steve Hydon, clinical professor in field education, explained the most common types and causes of stress social services professionals experience as a result of their work, and offered actionable tips for mitigating stress and promoting mental wellbeing. Understanding secondary trauma “Among the most common negative mental health effects of dealing with others’ trauma every day is secondary trauma,” said Hydon, whose expertise in secondary traumatic stress has informed research and intervention <cover>strategies for K-12 teachers across Los Angeles County. Secondary trauma, also referred to as vicarious trauma or compassion fatigue, is the indirect exposure to trauma through the reception of a firsthand account of another’s traumatic experience. According to the American Counseling Association, </cover>care providers may experience secondary trauma as a result of becoming “witnesses to the pain, fear, and terror that trauma survivors have endured.” This phenomenon can lead to a symptomology comparable to that of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), including reexperiencing, hyperarousal and avoidance. Clinicians who have experienced secondary trauma may find themselves unable to stop thinking about a certain client’s traumatic past, or alternatively, may find themselves purposefully avoiding the subject or even feeling numb toward "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"33/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1638929939#1_3233078716","relevance":2,"reason":"The document appears focused on stress and mental wellbeing for social services professionals rather than being dedicated specifically to vicarious trauma, but the passage directly defines secondary/vicarious trauma as indirect exposure through firsthand accounts of others' trauma, notes ACA language about witnessing survivors' pain/fear/terror, and mentions PTSD-like symptoms plus mitigation tips.","learned":"Secondary/vicarious trauma can be indirect exposure to trauma through receiving firsthand accounts of another person's traumatic experience, potentially causing PTSD-like reexperiencing, hyperarousal, avoidance, intrusive thoughts, or numbness."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1025","reason":"The American Counseling Association query may produce more authoritative or directly dedicated resources on vicarious trauma and coping, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1025"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_15_1638929939#2_3233081596","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1025","rank":2,"snippet":"for practicing effective self-care 1. Practice self-awareness 2. Divide the mission of self-care into categories 3. Create a detailed self-care plan 4. Stay accountable Creating increased awareness about the stress of social work \" , \"segment\": \"Understanding secondary trauma “Among the most common negative mental health effects of dealing with others’ trauma every day is secondary trauma,” said Hydon, whose expertise in secondary traumatic stress has informed research and intervention <cover>strategies for K-12 teachers across Los Angeles County. Secondary trauma, also referred to as vicarious trauma or compassion fatigue, is the indirect exposure to trauma through the reception of a firsthand account of another’s traumatic experience. According to the American Counseling Association, </cover>care providers may experience secondary trauma as a result of becoming “witnesses to the pain, fear, and terror that trauma survivors have endured.” This phenomenon can lead to a symptomology comparable to that of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), including reexperiencing, hyperarousal and avoidance. Clinicians who have experienced secondary trauma may find themselves unable to stop thinking about a certain client’s traumatic past, or alternatively, may find themselves purposefully avoiding the subject or even feeling numb toward "}}
