{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_1467297918#4_3111565923","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from a dedicated 'Vicarious trauma & burnout' page, with sections on impacts, recognizing signs/symptoms, protecting yourself, and prevention; the passage gives direct coping advice including reaching out for support, EAP/counselling, breaks, rest, play, and self-compassion.","learned":"Coping/protection strategies include support from managers, friends, colleagues, counsellors, EAPs or helplines; physical/mental breaks; rest; playful activities; and self-compassion."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"social workers\" (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies support) (+ signs symptoms definition))","reason":"Search a profession-specific angle for social workers, who are commonly exposed to vicarious trauma, to find additional dedicated resources with definitions, symptoms, and coping/self-care strategies from distinct sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"social workers\" (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies support) (+ signs symptoms definition))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689943621#0_2616465071","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1033","rank":3,"snippet":"than half of Americans report experiencing trauma, and 90% of people seeking mental health care have a trauma history. Social workers are on the front lines of the trauma fight. Their support can help survivors live meaningful lives after catastrophic events. But chronic exposure to other people’s suffering is itself a form of trauma. Social workers can suffer secondary or <cover>vicarious trauma, which may trigger burnout, stymie their ability to support clients, and trigger chronic mental health issues. Vicarious vs. Secondary Trauma in Social Work: What’s the Difference? Social workers inevitably work with people who have experienced trauma such as the death of a loved one, surviving a natural disaster, being in combat, living in an abusive marriage or family, or surviving a sexual assault. Over time, this traumatic exposure can cause social workers to experience symptoms </cover>of trauma such as anxiety, depression, and even posttraumatic stress (PTSD) . Secondary trauma is a traumatized reaction to another person’s experience.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"The Connection Between Social Work and Secondary Trauma\" , \"url\": \"https://www.goodtherapy.org/for-professionals/business-","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1688725936#0_2614437562","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1688725936#1_2614439973","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"42/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_41_1689943621#0_2616465071","relevance":2,"reason":"The document is closely related and appears dedicated to secondary/vicarious trauma in social work, directly explaining that chronic exposure to others' suffering can cause vicarious trauma and symptoms such as anxiety, depression, burnout, and PTSD; however, the visible passage does not yet show coping guidance, so it is incomplete for the full information need.","learned":"Social workers' chronic exposure to clients' suffering can itself become secondary/vicarious trauma, potentially causing burnout, impaired client support, anxiety, depression, PTSD, and chronic mental health issues."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1033","reason":"Continue the social-worker query because it is finding profession-specific vicarious/secondary trauma resources and may surface segments with coping, self-care, and support strategies."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1033"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#2_3617401534","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1033","rank":4,"snippet":"taken this trauma as your own, and it has triggered a shocking reaction in your emotions and actions. Vicarious Trauma typically involves a change in personal beliefs about the self and the world. Those in caring professions are most susceptible to experiencing Vicarious Trauma, including but not limited to the following jobs: Doctors, surgeons, nurses, personal <cover>support workers Counsellors, therapists, social workers Police officers, soldiers, rescue workers Lawyers, journalists, clergy, humanitarian workers In addition, if you have a close relationship with someone who has survived personal trauma, you may also experience Vicarious Trauma. Those with Vicarious Trauma are at risk of developing mental health issues because they might neglect their own issues. These issues might including anxiety, clinical depression, post-traumatic stress disorder, and addiction, among other problems. This is why it is important to address this trauma as soon as you can. Signs </cover>and Symptoms of Vicarious Trauma Vicarious trauma is directly related to a lack of work-life balance and the single experience of a traumatic event such as witnessing someone being tortured or threatened. This trauma affects how you think, feel, and act:\" , \"start_char\": 984.000000 , \"title"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"43/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1647729865#2_3617401534","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated CBT Psychology article on vicarious trauma and how to cope with it; it defines vicarious trauma, identifies susceptible professions including social workers, describes risks such as anxiety/depression/PTSD/addiction, and points to signs/symptoms and need to address it promptly, though it duplicates an earlier source.","learned":"Vicarious trauma can involve changes in beliefs about self and world, is common among caring/helping professions and close contacts of trauma survivors, and may contribute to anxiety, depression, PTSD, addiction, and work-life imbalance."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" wellbeing (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies support) (+ signs symptoms definition))","reason":"Search a broader wellbeing/self-care formulation to find additional distinct resources that define vicarious trauma and give practical coping or support strategies, avoiding over-reliance on repeated sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" wellbeing (+ coping \"self-care\" strategies support) (+ signs symptoms definition))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_1467297918#5_3111567834","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1034","rank":1,"snippet":"it’s important to maintain professional composure with your clients, emotional responses related to abuse and violence are natural and even appropriate. Staying in contact with how you feel and having self-compassion will help you to be resilient and sustain your work. Preventing burnout To help prevent the <cover>symptoms of vicarious trauma and burnout from escalating or happening in the first place: Utilise your team and managers for regular debriefing and other support Find out if you have access to an employee assistance program (known as EAP) to access in times of need Engage in reflective practice. This can be one-on-one with a trusted friend or your manager, counsellor or other support person (sometimes called ‘supervision’); with colleagues; or on your own e.g. by writing in a journal Honour your scheduled breaks and annual leave Evaluate your work space to ensure it is conducive to wellbeing – </cover>enough space for you and your colleagues to have lunch together, ‘chill-out’ spaces, lots of plants/flowers/colour/light. Be kind and supportive to your co-workers and make sure to celebrate achievements and birthdays to take time out\" , \"start_char\": 3864.000000"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_1467297918#5_3111567834","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment from the dedicated Lookout page on vicarious trauma and burnout, directly addressing prevention and coping through debriefing, manager/team support, EAP access, reflective practice or supervision, journaling, breaks/leave, wellbeing-oriented workspace, coworker support, and self-compassion.","learned":"Preventing escalation of vicarious trauma can involve regular debriefing, team/manager support, EAPs, reflective practice/supervision, journaling, honoring breaks and annual leave, supportive workspaces, kindness to coworkers, celebrating achievements, and self-compassion."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1034","reason":"The wellbeing query is yielding dedicated practical coping/prevention content for vicarious trauma, so continue to find any further unjudged grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1034"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_389221717#5_809170733","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1034","rank":2,"snippet":"\"Lifeline’s learnings Ann Evans MAPS, Practice Adviser, Service Support and Development, Lifeline Australia Working as a psychologist often entails exposure to potentially traumatic and distressing material, including clients’ stories. Regular exposure means that there is a significant risk of <cover>vicarious trauma and burnout. There is often an assumption that our training as psychologists inoculates us against these ill effects, however, over time, the cumulative effect of being exposed to traumatic or distressing material can take its toll, making helpers such as psychologists more at risk than the general population. The large amount of literature on compassion fatigue for various helpers also supports this notion (Figley, 1995). Recognising this and taking personal responsibility for self-care is essential for every psychologist. This is important not just for psychologists’ own wellbeing but also so that they are able to perform well in their role (Taylor & Furlonger, 2011; Figley, 1995). Evidence indicates that those who have been experiencing symptoms </cover>of secondary traumatisation or psychological distress, even if these are mild, will have difficulty in utilising their skills as well as those who are not distressed (Beaton & Murphy, 1995; Dunkley & Whelan, 2006). Due to the crisis nature of Lifeline ’s work, Lifeline’"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"45/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_389221717#5_809170733","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is directly relevant to vicarious trauma and burnout among psychologists, explaining cumulative exposure to traumatic client material and emphasizing personal self-care, but it appears more focused on professional wellbeing/Lifeline practice than being a dedicated answer to what vicarious trauma is and how to cope with it.","learned":"Psychologists and other helpers can experience cumulative vicarious trauma from repeated exposure to traumatic or distressing client material; self-care is essential both for wellbeing and for maintaining professional effectiveness."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"guide\" (+ cope coping \"self-care\" prevention) (+ signs symptoms definition))","reason":"With limited judgments remaining, search for guide-style pages explicitly combining vicarious trauma with definition/signs and coping/self-care/prevention to find additional dedicated grade-3 resources from distinct sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"vicarious trauma\" \"guide\" (+ cope coping \"self-care\" prevention) (+ signs symptoms definition))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_158990471#1_315201940","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1035","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_158990471#1_315201940\" , \"end_char\": 1802.000000 , \"headings\": \"Understand & Overcome Your Compassion Fatigue: An Online Guide Understand & Overcome Your Compassion Fatigue: An Online <cover>Guide Compassion Fatigue Basics The Process: Steps to Fatigue Clearing Up Terminology Compassion fatigue is synonymous with secondary traumatic stress: Compassion fatigue is not burnout: Compassion satisfaction is the opposite of compassion fatigue: Some symptoms of compassion fatigue mimic PTSD: Vicarious Trauma shares similar symptoms with compassion fatigue: Self-Assessment Questionnaire: Are You Vulnerable? Additional Warning Signs and Symptoms Developing a Self-Care </cover>Plan to Manage Your Compassion Fatigue How to Avoid Compassion Fatigue: Preventative Care 1. Know that putting yourself first isn’t selfish 2. Create emotional boundaries 3. Find your support network (Interview) A Nurse’s Experience with Compassion Fatigue 1. How/when did you first learn about secondary traumatic stress and compassion fatigue? 2. When did you know you were dealing with symptoms? 3. What are some steps you took to address compassion fatigue? 4. How long did it take to come out of that season? 5. How do you stay "}}
